metaTitle: Which Tombs in the Valley of the Kings Are Worth It (2026)
metaDescription: >-
  Your ticket covers three tombs out of around ten open. Which ones we choose,
  which extra tickets are worth it, 2026 prices, and why the answer changes.
---
Here is the thing nobody explains at the ticket booth: the Valley of the Kings is not one attraction. It is sixty-odd tombs, of which around ten are open on any given day, on a rotation that changes for conservation without much announcement. Your standard ticket admits you to **three of them**. Which three is entirely your choice, and almost everybody chooses badly, because they choose at the gate, in the heat, with no information.

Ahmed grew up twenty minutes from that gate and has been inside all of them. So let this save you the mistake people make every morning.

## The mistake almost everyone makes

Most visitors arrive between 9 and 11am, having taken a coach from Hurghada that left in the middle of the night. They are tired. In summer it is close to forty degrees in the ravine. Someone points at the two tombs nearest the entrance, usually **Ramesses IX (KV6)** and **Ramesses IV (KV2)**, because they are the closest and the queues move. KV2 happens to be a good choice. KV6 is only there because it is near.

Then they pay extra for **Tutankhamun (KV62)**, because it is the name they know.

And this is the honest part that costs us money to say: *Tutankhamun's tomb is, architecturally, one of the least impressive in the Valley.* It is small. It was probably not built for him; he died young and was buried in what may have been meant as a non-royal tomb. The treasure that made it famous is not in it: since November 2025 it has been shown at the Grand Egyptian Museum near the pyramids, more than five thousand objects together for the first time. What remains is the mummy in a glass case, his stone sarcophagus, one painted chamber, and a lot of people shuffling in a small space.

> People come out of Tutankhamun's tomb slightly embarrassed, as though they were supposed to feel something and did not.
>
> — Ahmed, on the West Bank

It is not that you shouldn't go. If standing two metres from the boy king matters to you, and for many people it genuinely does, go. Just go *knowing* what it is, and don't let it be the thing you spent your money and your energy on.

## The tombs actually worth your three

These are the ones [we take guests into](/experiences/valley-of-the-kings), when they are open. The Valley rotates, so treat this as a ranking rather than a fixed list.

- **KV11, Ramesses III — always.** Long, wide and covered end to end. The scene of the two blind harpers, which gave it its old nickname, is worth the ticket alone.
- **KV14, Tausert and Setnakht — always.** Enormous and strange: a queen's tomb, taken over by the king who came after her. Often quieter than its neighbours.
- **KV2, Ramesses IV — good.** Bright, colourful and easy on the legs. The one default choice that deserves its popularity.
- **KV6, Ramesses IX — skip, if you can.** Fine, and its ceiling has admirers, but most people are only in it because it is fifty metres from the gate.

![A royal tomb painted from floor to ceiling in the Valley of the Kings](/images/articles/valley-painted-ceiling.jpg)
*Some of these corridors are painted end to end, the colour still bright after three thousand years.*

### The extra tickets, ranked

- **Seti I (KV17) — if you do one thing.** Not a tomb so much as a cathedral cut downwards into rock: more than a hundred metres long, every surface carved and painted, the astronomical ceiling still holding its blue. It was closed for decades and reopened in 2016 with a separate ticket, EGP 2,000 (about €35), partly, it seems, to limit numbers.
- **Ramesses V/VI (KV9) — the best value.** Huge, painted from end to end, and only EGP 220 (about €4) on top. Most visitors don't know to ask for it.
- **Tutankhamun (KV62) — for the name.** EGP 700 (about €12). Go for the meaning, not the tomb.

If you are choosing between the extra ticket for Tutankhamun and the one for Seti I, and you can only justify one: **it is Seti I, and it is not close.**

![A king and a god face to face on a painted tomb pillar](/images/articles/valley-tomb-pillar.jpg)
*A king and a god, face to face. Look at the pillars as well as the walls.*

## When you go matters more than which you pick

You can choose the three best tombs in the Valley and still have a mediocre morning, because you arrived at ten with four coaches. The tombs are narrow. Forty people in a corridor built for a burial procession is not an experience; it is a queue with paintings.

The Valley opens at six. Between **six and about half past seven**, you can stand almost alone in KV11. By nine, you cannot. That hour and a half is worth more than any ticket upgrade you can buy.

- **Arrive at opening.** Not "early". At opening.
- **Bring a card.** The ticket office takes cards only, and the little tram from the visitor centre costs about EGP 20 extra.
- **Do the deepest tomb first,** while it is still cool and your legs are fresh. The descents are steeper than people expect.
- **Skip the middle of the day.** In the warm months the ravine holds heat like an oven between eleven and three, and the light is flat and harsh.
- **Come back in the late afternoon** for the West Bank temples, like [Hatshepsut's](/experiences/hatshepsut-temple), when the stone goes orange.

This is the single reason we build days the way we do. It is not that our guests get better tombs; anyone can buy the same ticket. It is that they get them *empty*.

## What most people miss entirely

Fifteen minutes from the Valley gate is **[Medinet Habu](/medinet-habu)**, the temple of Ramesses III, still holding its original paint, and on most mornings almost deserted. To our mind it is the most beautiful thing on the West Bank, and the majority of visitors never see it, because it is not on the coach itinerary. We wrote about [what Medinet Habu meant to the Egyptians](/insiders-guide/medinet-habu-meaning): they believed it was the place where the world began. Its pillars show Ramesses III as Osiris, and [his story with Isis](/insiders-guide/isis-and-osiris-love-story) is Egypt's oldest love story.

A few minutes from Medinet Habu is [the Valley of the Queens](/experiences/ramesseum-valley-of-queens). Its most famous tomb, **Nefertari's**, the finest painted surface in Egypt, has been closed for conservation since March 2024, with no reopening date announced. The valley itself is open, and quiet. If Nefertari reopens, we will know before you do.

![A carved sarcophagus lid in a royal tomb](/images/articles/valley-sarcophagus.jpg)
*Built to be hidden forever, and now waiting for you at six in the morning.*

If you have a day here, a real day, not a stop on a bus route, you can do the Valley properly at dawn, Medinet Habu at nine while it is still cool, and be drinking tea somewhere shaded before the heat arrives. That is not a luxury itinerary. It is just the correct order, which nobody tells you. [We can build the whole day around it](/concierge-day).
